# Refuse to resolve any version published less than this many days ago.
#
# Renovate's own `minimumReleaseAge` cooldowns (renovate.json) are applied when
# it *looks up* a version, so they only ever governed the direct dependency a PR
# names. Two paths walk straight around them:
#
#   - lock file maintenance deletes the lockfile and asks npm to rebuild it.
#     There is no lookup, so npm takes whatever is newest — Renovate's docs say
#     outright that this update type cannot validate minimum release age. Those
#     PRs auto-merge, so that is the highest-volume path to production and the
#     one with no cooldown on it.
#   - transitive dependencies, even in an ordinary bump: Renovate picks the
#     version of the direct package, npm resolves everything underneath it.
#
# npm enforces this one at resolution time instead, which covers both. Set to
# match the shortest Renovate cooldown (patch, 5 days) so npm never admits
# something renovate.json would have held back; renovate.test asserts they stay
# in step.
#
# Only affects resolution — `npm ci` installs from the lockfile without
# resolving, so CI and Vercel builds are unchanged. It bites where we want it
# to: Renovate regenerating the lockfile, and `npm install` locally.
#
# The option landed in npm 11.10.0 and is silently ignored before that
# ("Unknown project config"), so the floor is declared in two places rather
# than assumed from the Node major: engines.npm in package.json, and renovate.json's
# `constraints.npm` for the npm Renovate itself runs when it regenerates the
# lockfile. Node bundles vary within a major — 24.12.0 ships npm 11.6.2 (no),
# 24.14.1 ships 11.11.0 (yes) — so "Node 24" alone does not guarantee it.
#
# If the window ever blocks an `npm audit fix`, npm keeps the vulnerable
# version and exits non-zero rather than failing quietly — add the package to
# min-release-age-exclude to take the fix immediately.
min-release-age=5
